# Docker does not read .gitignore — everything excluded from the build context
# must be listed here. Keep in sync with .gitignore where it matters.
#
# The gobuilder stage does `COPY ./ ./`, so anything left in the context lands in
# that stage and any change to it invalidates the COPY layer, forcing
# `go mod vendor` + `go build` to rerun. Excluding build-irrelevant paths keeps
# the cache warm and the context upload small.
#
# Must NOT be excluded (the build needs them): cmd/, internal/, pkg/, scripts/,
# go.mod, go.sum. In particular internal/pkg/certificates/ backs the
# `//go:embed pks1/*.bin` and `pks2/*.bin` directives.

# git metadata — unused by the build
.git
**/.gitignore
.gitattributes

# editor / OS cruft
.idea/
.vscode/
**/.DS_Store

# temporary folder
tmp/

# build folder
build/

# original asn1 definitions
asn1/

# vendored deps — the gobuilder stage runs `go mod vendor` itself
vendor/

# shell test fixtures and docs — not part of the Go build
test/
docs/
*.md

# Build tooling — describes how to build, never an input to what gets built.
#
# Excluding these is not what makes the build work; it is what keeps the cache
# warm. Because the gobuilder stage does `COPY ./ ./`, that layer's cache key
# covers every file left in the context — so editing any of these would
# otherwise force a full `go mod vendor` + `go build`:
#   - Makefile: IMAGE_TAG is bumped on every release
#   - Dockerfile: edits below the COPY line (ENTRYPOINT, apt packages, ...)
#     should not invalidate the Go build above it
#
# Safe to exclude the Dockerfile and .dockerignore themselves: the builder
# receives both out-of-band, so an ignore rule cannot hide them from the build.
# It only stops them being COPY'd into a stage.
# https://docs.docker.com/build/concepts/context/
**/Dockerfile
.dockerignore
Makefile
build-binaries-prod.sh
